2️⃣ Abstraction — "You Don't Need to Know HOW, Just WHAT" 🎭

The idea: Hide the COMPLEX implementation details, show only the essential features. This is done using abstract classes or interfaces.

Real analogy: When you use your phone's camera app 📸, you press ONE button "capture." You have ZERO idea about the sensor algorithms, image processing pipelines, autofocus math happening behind that button. You don't need to! That's abstraction — complexity hidden, simple interface exposed.

🔥 Simplest Abstraction Example
abstract class Animal {
    abstract void sound();
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog();
        d.sound();
    }
}
🧠 How to explain it to the interviewer

Say:

"Abstraction means hiding the implementation details and showing only the required functionality. Here, the Animal class only says that every animal should have a sound() method, but it doesn't tell how the sound is made. The Dog class provides the actual implementation by printing 'Dog barks'."
⚠️ Encapsulation vs Abstraction — the #1 confusion:

Encapsulation = HIDING data (the "how it's protected") 🔒
Abstraction = HIDING complexity (the "how it's simplified") 🎭

Say this in interviews and you'll sound sharp: "Encapsulation is about data hiding for security/control, abstraction is about hiding implementation complexity to simplify usage."